# gotime configuration — worked example.
#
# Copy to ~/.config/gotime/config and edit, or run `gotime --init` to generate
# a bare starter seeded with the work trees this machine already has.
#
# Sourced as bash, so $HOME and command substitution work here. Every key is
# also an environment variable, and the environment wins over this file — which
# is what makes `GOTIME_CMD=vim gotime` a one-off rather than an edit.

# Trees to scan for workspaces. Entries are "[LABEL:]path", separated by
# whitespace or newlines; the LABEL becomes the menu section heading, and
# without one the directory's own name is used (so "$HOME/projects" alone
# heads its section PROJECTS). Paths must not contain spaces.
#
# One line per root reads better once there are more than two:
GOTIME_ROOTS="COURSES:$HOME/courseware
PROJECTS:$HOME/projects
SCRATCH:$HOME/tmp/experiments"

# An always-present first window, opened here and running GOTIME_CMD. This is
# the second context you want in every session regardless of what you picked —
# a notes vault, a dotfiles repo, an issue tracker checkout. Leave it empty for
# no pinned window, and gotime builds a two-window session instead.
#
# The label is the tmux window name, so keep it short:
GOTIME_PIN="notes:$HOME/vaults/notes"

# tmux session name. Change it if you want more than one gotime session alive
# at a time — GOTIME_SESSION=side gotime some-project.
GOTIME_SESSION="gotime"

# Command started in the pinned window and the selected window. The "local"
# window never runs it; that one is always a plain shell in ~.
GOTIME_CMD="claude"
